---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { AUTHOR_NAME, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<Layout
	title="About"
	description="How DayAlmanac decides what a date is, where its date tables come from, and why some entries say plainly that nobody knows who started an observance."
>
	<div class="mx-auto max-w-[680px]">
		<nav class="mb-8 text-sm text-[var(--ink)]/40">
			<a href="/" class="no-underline hover:text-[var(--ink)] hover:underline">Home</a>
		</nav>

		<h1 class="font-display m-0 text-[2.1rem] leading-tight font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">About {SITE_TITLE}</h1>

		<div class="font-prose mt-7 text-[1.05rem] leading-[1.85] text-[var(--ink)]/85">
			<p class="mb-5">
				{SITE_TITLE} answers three questions about a day: when it falls, what it is, and who
				started it. Calendar sites are usually confident about the first and vague about the
				third, and the vagueness is where most of the errors live.
			</p>

			<h2 class="font-display mt-10 mb-3 text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Where the dates come from</h2>
			<p class="mb-5">
				Dates on this site are not typed in by hand. Where an observance follows a stateable
				Gregorian rule (a fixed calendar date, the nth weekday of a month, a fixed offset from
				one of those), the table is generated from that rule by a script, and the script is
				covered by unit tests whose expected values are transcribed from published authorities
				rather than from the script's own output. A test that checks code against itself passes
				by construction and catches nothing.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-5">
				Where an observance follows a lunisolar or religious calendar (Easter, the Hebrew and
				Islamic calendars, the Chinese calendar), no conversion is implemented here at all. Those
				dates are transcribed from a published authority and cited on the page. A calendar
				conversion that is subtly wrong four years out is worse than a short table with a source
				next to it, because nothing would reveal the error until someone had already planned
				around it.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-5">
				Every date table runs at least five years forward. That is partly a convenience and
				partly a safety margin: if an annual refresh is ever missed, the page degrades slowly
				instead of going wrong all at once.
			</p>

			<h2 class="font-display mt-10 mb-3 text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Why some entries say "nobody knows"</h2>
			<p class="mb-5">
				A large share of the observances people search for were not proclaimed by anyone. They
				were registered by a company with something to sell, started by one person on social
				media, or simply appeared on a list and propagated. The convention elsewhere is to cover
				this with a passive phrase, something like "has been celebrated since the early 2010s," which reads like
				history and is not.
			</p>
			<p class="mb-5">
				Here the founding of an observance is a required field with two states: traced to a
				named source, or not traced. When it is traced, the proclamation, registration or
				founder is named and linked. When it is not, the page says so in as many words. An
				honest gap is more useful than an invented origin, and it is the one thing a reader
				cannot get from the sites that rank above this one.
			</p>

			<h2 class="font-display mt-10 mb-3 text-[1.4rem] font-semibold text-[var(--ink)]">Corrections</h2>
			<p class="mb-5">
				If a date here disagrees with a primary source, the primary source wins and the page gets
				fixed. Corrections go to <a class="text-[var(--flag-dark)] underline underline-offset-2" href="mailto:contact@dayalmanac.com">contact@dayalmanac.com</a>.
			</p>

			<p class="m-0 border-t border-[var(--rule)] pt-5 text-[0.95rem] text-[var(--ink)]/60">
				Written and edited by the {AUTHOR_NAME}.
			</p>
		</div>
	</div>
</Layout>
